---
/**
 * Terms and conditions.
 *
 * The substantive clause is the accuracy one, and it is placed third rather than
 * buried: people book non-refundable travel on the dates this site produces,
 * many festival dates across the calendars it carries are set by lunar
 * observation, and national notifications sometimes land weeks before the day.
 * A disclaimer that only appears in a liability paragraph at the bottom does
 * not do that job.
 *
 * Governing law is India and the operator is an individual, per the site owner.
 * No company name is claimed anywhere, because there is no company.
 *
 * The IP clause deliberately does not claim ownership of holiday dates. They are
 * facts published in government gazettes, and asserting copyright over them
 * would be both wrong and unenforceable.
 */
import DocPage from "../layouts/DocPage.astro";

const TITLE = "Terms & Conditions | The Long Weekends";
const DESCRIPTION =
  "Terms of use for The Long Weekends. A free informational holiday calculator — verify dates against your employer's official list before booking travel.";
---

<DocPage
  title={TITLE}
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/terms"
  eyebrow="Legal"
  heading="Terms & conditions"
  lede="A free tool that suggests when to take leave. Useful, but not authoritative — confirm any date against your employer's official holiday list before you book anything you cannot cancel."
  updated="2026-08-24"
  numbered
>
  <h2>Agreement</h2>
  <p>
    These terms govern your use of thelongweekends.com (the "Site"), operated by an
    individual based in India ("we", "us"). By using the Site you accept them. If you
    do not, please stop using it — there is no account to close and nothing to
    cancel.
  </p>

  <h2>What the Site is</h2>
  <p>
    The Site is a free calculator. It takes published public-holiday dates, works out
    which combinations of weekends, holidays and leave days produce the longest
    unbroken breaks, and shows you the results. That is the entire service.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is provided at no charge, with no account, and with no commitment that it will
    remain available. We may change, restrict or discontinue it at any time without
    notice.
  </p>

  <h2>Accuracy, and why you must check</h2>
  <p>
    This is the clause that matters, so it is not buried at the bottom. <strong>Holiday dates on this Site may be wrong or out of date.</strong> Several
    reasons, all of them ordinary:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Many festival dates — Eid, Diwali, Vesak, the lunar new years — depend on
      observation and are confirmed only shortly beforehand.
    </li>
    <li>
      Governments issue and amend holiday notifications on their own schedules,
      sometimes only weeks before the date, and some countries had not published
      their 2027 list when this data was compiled.
    </li>
    <li>
      Only nationwide holidays are listed. Where a country’s holidays vary from
      one part of it to another, the days you actually get may differ.
    </li>
    <li>
      Employers are not obliged to follow the government list. Your office may
      observe different days, treat some as restricted or optional, or require
      particular days to be worked.
    </li>
    <li>
      Dates are transcribed by hand from gazette notifications, and transcription can
      introduce mistakes.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>
      Before booking travel, applying for leave, or making any commitment you cannot
      reverse, verify every date against your employer's official holiday list and the
      relevant government notification.
    </strong> Treat this Site as a planning aid, never as the authority. If you find an
    incorrect date, please <a href="/contact">report it</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>No professional advice</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing here is legal, employment, financial or travel advice. The Site does not
    know your leave entitlement, your contract, your company's leave policy or your
    employer's approval process, and its suggestions carry no implication that any
    leave request will be granted.
  </p>

